<Project>

  <Import Project="$([MSBuild]::GetPathOfFileAbove('Directory.Build.props', '$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../'))" />

  <PropertyGroup Label="Library defaults">
    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <IncludeSymbols>true</IncludeSymbols>
    <SymbolPackageFormat>snupkg</SymbolPackageFormat>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    VersionPrefix, not Version, and THE VALUE IS THE VERSION BEING PREPARED rather than the one last
    released. publish.yml packs a GitHub Release with no suffix and every other trigger with
    `-p:VersionSuffix=...`, so a push that passes CI on master publishes 0.2.0-ci.N and a published
    release publishes plain 0.2.0.

    Leaving this at a version already released is the trap worth naming: NuGet orders every
    prerelease of a version BELOW the release of that version, so 0.2.0-ci.N builds published after
    0.2.0 shipped could never resolve as the latest. They would be permanently invisible and would
    occupy permanent public version slots, because nuget.org has no delete. Cutting a release is
    therefore two steps: publish the release, then bump this.

    One VersionPrefix across all four packages is also what keeps the set consistent. `dotnet pack`
    of a project with a ProjectReference emits a dependency on the referenced package at the version
    it was built with, so MassiveDotNet.Rest 0.2.0 depends on MassiveDotNet >= 0.2.0, and all four
    are published together from one run.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Label="Package identity">
    <VersionPrefix>0.3.0</VersionPrefix>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <!-- An SPDX expression rather than PackageLicenseFile: NuGet renders it as a link to the
         canonical licence text, so a client reads the terms without unpacking anything. The LICENSE
         file is packed as well, below, so the package is self-contained either way.

         No PackageIcon. #19 lists an icon in scope, and this deliberately does not ship one:
         nothing here is branded, and an invented mark would only have to be replaced later, which
         costs a package page that changes identity between versions. fmpdotnet made the same call
         for the same reason. Adding one later is a property and a file, not a redesign. -->
    <PackageLicenseExpression>MIT</PackageLicenseExpression>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="/" />
    <None Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../LICENSE" Pack="true" PackagePath="/" Visible="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Debuggability of a shipped build. A .snupkg carries the PDBs separately, so the main package
    stays small and a consumer who never steps into the SDK downloads nothing extra. Source Link is
    what makes those PDBs useful: it stamps the commit into the PDB so a debugger can fetch the
    exact source for the binary being stepped through.

    It needs no PackageReference. The .NET SDK has carried Microsoft.SourceLink.GitHub in-box since
    .NET 8 and resolves the provider from the git remote, which is the reason rule 7's cap on core's
    dependencies is untouched by any of this.

    EmbedUntrackedSources is load-bearing here rather than incidental. Every REST model is
    deserialized through code the JSON source generator emits from MassiveJsonContext, and the 199
    committed .g.cs files are in git but the source-generated ones are not, so without this,
    stepping into deserialization reaches a file Source Link cannot resolve.

    ContinuousIntegrationBuild is set at the root, conditioned on CI, and deliberately not here: it
    normalises source paths so the same commit packs to the same bytes anywhere, which is what makes
    a build reproducible, and also what would replace a local developer's real paths with
    placeholders and break stepping into a local `dotnet pack`.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Label="Public API lock">
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.PublicApiAnalyzers" PrivateAssets="all" IncludeAssets="runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive" />
    <AdditionalFiles Include="PublicAPI.Shipped.txt" />
    <AdditionalFiles Include="PublicAPI.Unshipped.txt" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <PropertyGroup Label="Source Link">
    <PublishRepositoryUrl>true</PublishRepositoryUrl>
    <EmbedUntrackedSources>true</EmbedUntrackedSources>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup Label="AOT and trimming">
    <IsAotCompatible>true</IsAotCompatible>
    <IsTrimmable>true</IsTrimmable>
    <EnableTrimAnalyzer>true</EnableTrimAnalyzer>
    <EnableAotAnalyzer>true</EnableAotAnalyzer>
    <EnableSingleFileAnalyzer>true</EnableSingleFileAnalyzer>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup Label="System.Text.Json - source generation only, no reflection fallback">
    <JsonSerializerIsReflectionEnabledByDefault>false</JsonSerializerIsReflectionEnabledByDefault>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
